<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#shreni">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shreni</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">6th century BCE–12th century CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> · Also known as śreṇi, seni</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A shreni was an association of traders, merchants or artisans in ancient and early medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, usually one for each vocation, which acted as an economic body, a largely self-governing community and, within limits, a tribunal for its members. Commonly translated as '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guild</a>', though scholars caution against the comparison with European guilds, shrenis are attested from the 6th century BCE to the 12th century CE and reached their height under the Mauryan and Gupta empires. Wealthy shrenis lent money and kept religious endowments; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> carvers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vidisha">Vidisha</a> are credited with sponsoring the southern gateway of the stupa at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanchi">Sanchi</a>. Their hereditary crafts may have fed into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> system.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The word śreṇi comes from a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> term meaning a row, line or series, and by extension a body of people following a common pursuit; in legal usage it came to mean an occupational corporation. Related terms such as gaṇa, pūga, nigama and saṃgha appear in some periods and regions. The roots of the institution are usually traced to the later Vedic period, whose texts mention groups of weavers, carpenters and smiths. These early groups were informal, but they set patterns of cooperation, division of labour and hereditary specialisation from which formal bodies grew.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>By the 6th and 5th centuries BCE, as towns grew, trade routes lengthened and money spread through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a> valley, shrenis appear as recognisable institutions. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panini">Pāṇini</a> and the Jātaka tales mention them, and several shrenis are recorded as converting together to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jainism</a>, faiths with strong support among the urban merchant class. Kautilya's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthashastra">Arthashastra</a> treats them as a normal part of economic life and notes that even rulers might borrow from them.</p><p>Under the Gupta empire shreni heads, called śreṣṭhins, and caravan leaders sat on town and district councils, and powerful shrenis set standards for their trades and financed religious and public works. The great merchant corporations of the south traded widely across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> in post-Gupta times, but from about the end of the first millennium CE the classical shrenis declined as political structures and patterns of trade changed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>A shreni was usually led by a head, called jeṭṭhaka or pamukha in Buddhist sources and pradhāna in others, who held office by heredity or by election of the members, assisted by a council of senior members and by clerks, the kāyasthas. A head found guilty of misconduct could be removed or punished. Shrenis procured raw materials, organised production, controlled quality, fixed wages and prices and found markets, and they judged their members under their own customary law, shreni-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharma">dharma</a>, which the Dharmaśāstra tradition recognised; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manusmriti">Manusmriti</a> tells the king to inquire into the law of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a>.</p><p>Scholars suggest that keeping the traditions and trade secrets of a craft within a shreni through endogamy may have fed into the strict <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> endogamy of later times. The chief evidence is the 9th-century Siyadoni inscription, which follows a salt dealer whose father had followed the same trade; by its end the family is described as members of the nemaka-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jati">jati</a>, the salt-dealer caste.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Shreni” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Traders and craftsmen formed guilds (<b>shrenis</b>) with their own laws, which the Manusmriti told kings to respect.</blockquote><a href="/societies/classical-india">Social Hierarchy of Classical India</a></li></ul></section>
